# Docker BuildKit reads `.dockerignore` and never this file (podman/buildah read
# both), so `.dockerignore` beside it is an identical copy — a copy rather than a
# symlink because not every builder follows one. `task lint:ignore-files` (part
# of `lint:check`, so CI) fails the moment the two drift.
#
# What never belongs in a build context (design §14.4, D17).
#
# The first block is the one that matters: `dev/secrets/` holds the VAPID and
# federation private keys a developer minted for their own machine (§12.5). They are
# gitignored, so they are invisible to a `git archive` — and a container build reads
# the working tree, not the index. Without this line a `podman build` on a developer
# machine bakes their signing keys into an image layer, where `docker history` finds
# them long after the file was deleted.
dev/secrets/
dev/.env.local
dev/dex/config.local.yaml
dev/dex/env.local
web/.env.local
config/local.toml

# Build outputs. Copying a host `target/` into the context does not just cost
# minutes — it lands artefacts built against another glibc next to the ones the
# builder makes.
target/
# Its own cargo workspace (task fuzz), not part of the image; cargo-chef would read its manifest.
fuzz/
# The local knowledge graph (graphify), 20+ MB the build never reads.
graphify-out/
web/dist/
web/coverage/
docs/.next/
docs/out/
**/node_modules/
# The web image builds from `web/` as its own context; nothing here reads it, and
# `web/src-tauri/target/` alone is gigabytes a local build would otherwise send.
web/

# Version control and tooling state.
.git/
.github/
.forgejo/
.task/
**/.DS_Store

# Documentation and local scaffolding: none of it is read by either build, and all
# of it invalidates layer caches when it changes.
docs/
*.md
!README.md
